# Line-ending policy (#393).
#
# The repo had no .gitattributes and `core.autocrlf=false`, so whichever
# endings a file happened to be committed with became that file's endings
# forever: 554 files LF, 114 CRLF, and 7 with both *inside the same file*.
#
# That is not cosmetic. Any tool that rewrites a whole file — a formatter, a
# codemod, an editor with a different default — silently flips that file's
# endings and turns a three-line change into a full-file rewrite. PR #392 saw
# a 6131-insertion diff for ~510 real insertions, and #319 and #370 collided
# on whole-file conflicts that had to be resolved by hand. With several agent
# sessions working this repo in parallel, that is a recurring conflict source,
# not a one-off.
#
# So: one convention, LF, enforced by git rather than by each contributor's
# editor settings. `text=auto` lets git decide what is text; `eol=lf` pins
# what lands in the working tree and the object database.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary formats. Listed explicitly rather than relying on `text=auto`'s
# heuristic: a false "this is text" guess on a binary file corrupts it on
# checkout, and the heuristic only inspects the first 8000 bytes.
*.png   binary
*.ico   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.eot   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.webp  binary
*.mp4   binary
*.webm  binary

# SVGs are text and want LF like everything else — they're hand-edited and
# diffed here (see `svgo.config.mjs` and the optimize:svg script), so treating
# them as opaque blobs would lose that.
*.svg   text eol=lf

# Windows-only shells. `.ps1` runs fine with LF under both Windows PowerShell
# 5.1 and pwsh, so it follows the same rule; `.bat`/`.cmd` genuinely need CRLF
# — cmd.exe can mis-parse LF-only batch files at line boundaries.
*.bat   text eol=crlf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf
